{"causes": ["Persistent and devastating Wokou (Japanese pirate) raids on Korean coastal areas destabilized trade and security throughout the Goryeo and early Joseon periods.", "The Joseon Dynasty, under King Sejong, sought to assert its sovereignty and protect its maritime borders from external threats, viewing Tsushima Island as a primary pirate stronghold.", "Political instability and the perceived inability of the Japanese central government to control its regional daimyo contributed to the proliferation of piracy emanating from islands like Tsushima."], "effects": ["The Ōei Invasion led to a significant, albeit temporary, reduction in Wokou piracy along the Korean coastline for several decades.", "The campaign contributed directly to the establishment of more formalized diplomatic and trade relations between Joseon and Japan, culminating in the Gyeyhae Treaty of 1443.", "Yi Chongmu's victory enhanced Joseon's strategic confidence in its naval capabilities and established a precedent for proactive maritime defense in East Asia."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "king-sejong", "sourceName": "King Sejong the Great", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "yi-chongmu", "targetName": "Yi Chongmu", "context": "King Sejong commissioned Yi Chongmu to lead the critical Ōei Invasion of Tsushima Island in 1419."}, {"sourceSlug": "oei-invasion", "sourceName": "Ōei Invasion", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "tsushima-island", "targetName": "Tsushima Island", "context": "The Ōei Invasion, commanded by Yi Chongmu, took place on Tsushima Island in 1419, targeting Wokou pirate bases."}, {"sourceSlug": "yi-chongmu", "sourceName": "Yi Chongmu", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "oei-invasion", "targetName": "Ōei Invasion", "context": "Yi Chongmu commanded the Joseon naval forces during the successful Ōei Invasion against Japanese pirates in 1419."}, {"sourceSlug": "yi-chongmu", "sourceName": "Yi Chongmu", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "gyeyhae-treaty", "targetName": "Gyeyhae Treaty", "context": "The success of Yi Chongmu's military campaign contributed to the diplomatic conditions that led to the Gyeyhae Treaty of 1443, regulating Joseon-Japan trade."}, {"sourceSlug": "joseon-dynasty", "sourceName": "Joseon Dynasty", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "yi-chongmu", "targetName": "Yi Chongmu", "context": "Yi Chongmu served as a prominent general within the military structure of the early Joseon Dynasty."}], "places": [{"name": "Tsushima Island, Japan", "role": "Site of major invasion"}, {"name": "Hanseong (Seoul), Korea", "role": "Capital of Joseon Dynasty"}, {"name": "Gyeongsang Province, Korea", "role": "Base for naval operations"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Yi Chongmu", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "yi-chongmu"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q484608", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yi_Chongmu"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Yi Chongmu's leadership of the Ōei Invasion in 1419 significantly curtailed Wokou piracy for decades and laid the groundwork for the Gyeyhae Treaty, fundamentally altering Joseon-Japan relations and maritime security in the region. His actions established a precedent for proactive military engagement by the Joseon Dynasty.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T10:26:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34751516989", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Yi Chongmu (1360–1425) was a Korean general who led the Ōei Invasion of Tsushima Island in 1419.", "newValue": "Yi Chongmu (1360–1425) was a pivotal Korean general of the early Joseon Dynasty, most renowned for leading the decisive Ōei Invasion of Tsushima Island in 1419. His military prowess and strategic execution under King Sejong's reign were instrumental in temporarily quelling the persistent threat of Japanese pirates, known as Wokou, who had plagued the Korean coastline for centuries. This campaign marked a rare and significant proactive military intervention by Joseon beyond its immediate borders, aimed at securing maritime stability and asserting regional influence.

In 1419, following years of escalating Wokou raids that disrupted trade and terrorized coastal communities, King Sejong authorized a large-scale punitive expedition against Tsushima, a primary base for these pirates. Yi Chongmu was entrusted with commanding a massive fleet of 227 ships and approximately 17,000 soldiers. The Joseon forces launched a swift and devastating attack, landing on Tsushima Island, burning pirate strongholds, seizing ships, and liberating Korean captives. While not a permanent occupation, the invasion inflicted significant damage on the pirate infrastructure and compelled the island's daimyo, Sō Sadamori, to acknowledge Joseon's suzerainty and cooperate in suppressing piracy.

The Ōei Invasion, largely orchestrated and executed by Yi Chongmu, had profound and lasting consequences for Joseon's foreign policy and maritime security. It effectively curtailed large-scale Wokou incursions for several decades, allowing for a period of relative peace and prosperity along the Korean coast. Furthermore, the successful campaign paved the way for the formalization of diplomatic and trade relations between Joseon and Japan, culminating in the Gyeyhae Treaty of 1443. This agreement established designated ports for Japanese trade and regulated Japanese settlement in Korea, stabilizing a previously volatile relationship."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-13T10:26:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34751516989", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1360, Korea"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1360, Korea"}], "newValue": ["Persistent and devastating Wokou (Japanese pirate) raids on Korean coastal areas destabilized trade and security throughout the Goryeo and early Joseon periods.", "The Joseon Dynasty, under King Sejong, sought to assert its sovereignty and protect its maritime borders from external threats, viewing Tsushima Island as a primary pirate stronghold.", "Political instability and the perceived inability of the Japanese central government to control its regional daimyo contributed to the proliferation of piracy emanating from islands like Tsushima."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-13T10:26:55Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34751516989", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1425"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1425"}], "newValue":